EQUATION INPUTS (when any interaction has type "equation_input") — the scaffold owns parsing and re-sampling; do not hand-roll either:
  1. Call setPlotFrame({ xDomain, yDomain, margin }) first, then use plotX / plotY for every coordinate.
  2. Create a plot handle per field with plotFunction / fillBetween / plotParametric, using the field's
     "domain" AND its "variables" (e.g. plotFunction(id, { expr: field.default, variables: field.variables,
     domain: field.domain })) — the constructor compiles the expression immediately and throws on an
     undeclared variable.
  3. Wire each input with bindEquationInput('{field.id}Input', handle, { variables: field.variables }) — this
     bare-handle form only works for plotFunction (bindEquationInput defaults to calling .setFn on the target,
     and only plotFunction has that method). For a fillBetween boundary pass { handle, method: 'setLower' } or
     { handle, method: 'setUpper' }; for a plotParametric component pass { handle, method: 'setX' } or
     { handle, method: 'setY' } — fillBetween and plotParametric have no .setFn/.setExpr and a bare handle
     would throw.
  4. NEVER hardcode the source figure's topology and re-solve only a few corner/scalar values. If the
     source shows a straight-edged region, the drawn path must STILL come from sampling the compiled function,
     so typing a curved expression produces a curved result. A polygon built from a fixed list
     of corner points is a failure even when those corners are recomputed.
  5. NEVER wrap evaluation in a try/catch that silently restores a default expression or default geometry.
     bindEquationInput already keeps the last good curve and shows the parse error inline; adding your own
     fallback is what makes edits appear to do nothing.
  6. Recompute every derived answer the figure reports (e.g. an integral value) with integrateSimpson over
     the same compiled function, so the number and the picture can never disagree.

CODE EDITOR (when an interaction has type "code_editor") — a trace-and-playback workbench, not a run-once box. The student writes algorithm logic; every mark the figure draws comes from a recorded frame:
  1. TRACE API — implement each op in the interaction's "api" so it appends a frame BEFORE returning its value.
     A frame is { kind, state, indices, message }: kind = which op ran (drives caption wording and highlight
     style), state = a deep copy of the data at that instant, indices = the elements the op touched (highlight
     exactly these), message = one human-readable line ("compare A[3]=2 with A[7]=14 → false"). Freeze the API
     object, range-check every index, and expose no reference to the underlying data — mutation reachable only
     through an op is what makes the replay gap-free.
  2. SANDBOX — run student code in a Web Worker built from a Blob URL (revoke it when done), with a wall-clock
     timeout (~1–2s) AND hard caps on op count and frame count, so a runaway loop is reported as an error rather
     than hanging the page. Wrap the code so a missing or misnamed "entry_point" gives a clear message. Show
     syntax errors, thrown errors, timeouts and cap hits inline as the final frame, never as a silent no-op.
     Never eval student code on the main thread.
  3. EDITOR UI — <textarea> seeded with "sample_code"; tabs to swap in "buggy_code" and a bare starter; an
     editable field for the interaction's "input"; a Run button that disables while running. Put all of it in
     #ui and let the scaffold dock the panel: it auto-detects a textarea and turns #ui from a floating overlay
     into a real side column, shrinking #container to fit beside it and firing 'fig-resize' so the figure
     re-lays out. Do NOT restyle #ui's position/width/max-height and do not build your own side panel — call
     setUiLayout('right', 420) only if you want a different width.
  4. PLAYBACK — first / prev / play-pause / next, a speed control, an "n of N" counter, the current frame's
     message as a caption, and a running log. Draw the structure by redrawing from frame[i] alone: scrubbing
     backwards must look identical to arriving there forwards, so do not accumulate SVG elements across frames
     (reuse a group you clear, or update existing shapes in place).
  5. VERDICT — test the final state against "success_check" and show a status readout distinguishing
     not-run / running / correct / wrong answer / error. On a wrong answer, highlight the elements that violate
     the property so the buggy sample is diagnosable, not just marked wrong.

